WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:02.713
The Invisible Disaster In 1986 the town of Pripyat, in what is now Ukraine, was a thriving community

2
00:00:02.714 --> 00:00:12.781
with good schools and the first real supermarket in the country. Its residents, who were mostly employed at the

3
00:00:12.782 --> 00:00:20.511
nearby Chernobyl nuclear plant, took great pride in their flourishing town. But on the morning of Saturday, April

4
00:00:20.512 --> 00:00:30.192
26, the people of Pripyat awoke to startling news: a section of the Chernobyl plant had exploded during

5
00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:37.110
the night. Although the residents were concerned about the plant, they continued with their activities as if it were

6
00:00:37.111 --> 00:00:44.001
an ordinary day. Adults ran errands and strolled outdoors in the sunshine; children rode bikes and played in

7
00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:53.491
the streets. Some people gathered on balconies, trying to catch a better glimpse of the strange, reddish glow

8
00:00:53.492 --> 00:01:02.745
coming from the plant. Many even described the glow as beautiful. Few were aware that the unusual color was

9
00:01:02.746 --> 00:01:09.824
actually a sign of danger—a danger that was about to change their lives forever. The next morning residents

10
00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:17.560
grew more alarmed when it was announced by radio that Pripyat had to be evacuated. Worries mounted when

11
00:01:17.561 --> 00:01:23.208
military men appeared on the streets, wearing face masks and rinsing the ground with white powder. Soon a long

12
00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:29.783
line of nearly two thousand buses arrived to take the residents away. The people of Pripyat assumed they'd

13
00:01:29.784 --> 00:01:37.784
be back in a few days, and so they left behind most of their belongings. What lurked in

14
00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:39.795
the streets of Pripyat? What invisible threat forced its residents to leave their homes and never return? A Nuclear

15
00:01:39.796 --> 00:01:45.031
Accident The explosion at the Chernobyl power plant was the worst nuclear accident in history. At the plant,

16
00:01:45.032 --> 00:01:55.109
machines called nuclear reactors were used to break apart uranium, releasing large amounts of energy. The energy was

17
00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:02.878
then used to make electricity. But this process also produced radiation, which has no taste or smell and cannot

18
00:02:02.879 --> 00:02:14.193
be seen by the human eye. If radiation is not properly contained or controlled, it can spread rapidly

19
00:02:14.194 --> 00:02:19.749
through the environment, damaging tissues and cells and making people and other living things very sick. A section

20
00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:27.332
of the Chernobyl nuclear plant blew up after a safety test went wrong. The accident released a huge amount

21
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:38.174
of this radiation into the atmosphere, creating the eerie glow seen from Pripyat. The fallout settled thickly over

22
00:02:38.175 --> 00:02:43.160
Ukraine as well as over Belarus and Russia, contaminating streets, buildings, fields, forests, and bodies of water. Winds

23
00:02:43.161 --> 00:02:49.477
blew more of the radioactive material over central and northern Europe. At the time many farms in Ukraine produced

24
00:02:49.478 --> 00:02:55.814
dairy products as well as vegetables, grains, and meat. These food products quickly absorbed the fallout, exposing many

25
00:02:55.815 --> 00:03:01.462
people to dangerous levels of radiation. Governments around the world had to ban certain foods in order to

26
00:03:01.463 --> 00:03:03.371
ensure people's safety. The Chernobylites Immediately after the explosion, workers from surrounding areas flooded in to control the disaster

27
00:03:03.372 --> 00:03:10.479
at the plant. These men, called liquidators, worked for only short amounts of time, trying to minimize their

28
00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:17.560
exposure. But some later said they tasted metal and felt prickly sensations. After the cleanup, hundreds of these

29
00:03:17.561 --> 00:03:25.369
men were hospitalized. Many suffered from radiation sickness that caused severe vomiting and sometimes death. Most of the workers

30
00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:34.688
who survived still suffer from chronic health problems. The thousands of people who had to leave their homes

31
00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:40.098
and possessions endured more hardships too. The Chernobylites—as they began to call themselves—faced discrimination from those who thought radiation

32
00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:47.773
could be contagious. In their new schools, relocated children were made fun of and excluded. One woman recalled

33
00:03:47.774 --> 00:03:52.681
how her son returned home from school in tears. No one wanted to sit next to him in

34
00:03:52.682 --> 00:04:02.344
class because he was "radioactive." Assessing the Damage It is estimated that over 300,000 people across Europe were forced

35
00:04:02.345 --> 00:04:04.354
to relocate as a result of Chernobyl. Millions continue to suffer from cancer, physical handicaps, financial hardship, and

36
00:04:04.355 --> 00:04:18.754
psychological trauma. Experts are still determining the extent of the damage. The government of the Soviet Union, criticized

37
00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:32.912
for both its secrecy about the accident and its slow response in places like Pripyat, reported that about thirty

38
00:04:32.913 --> 00:04:39.824
people had died. But many scientists believe that the total number of deaths was actually much higher—and will

39
00:04:39.825 --> 00:04:51.987
continue to rise. They point in particular to a sharp increase in cancer rates in the affected areas,

40
00:04:51.988 --> 00:04:58.883
with thyroid cancer, once a rare disease, affecting children in large and alarming numbers. There is also evidence that

41
00:04:58.884 --> 00:05:06.813
the radiation from Chernobyl caused birth defects, heart diseases, and other health problems. In parts of Belarus, Ukraine,

42
00:05:06.814 --> 00:05:12.138
and Russia, children were born with abnormal body parts and life-threatening diseases. Mental health problems such as depression

43
00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:19.961
and anxiety have been especially troublesome. Many feel as though Chernobyl has filled their lives with a sense of

44
00:05:19.962 --> 00:05:23.033
doom; they live in constant fear for the health of their family members. Nuclear Energy: A Controversy The

45
00:05:23.034 --> 00:05:28.194
Chernobyl disaster has led to a lot of controversy about nuclear energy. Those who support the use of

46
00:05:28.195 --> 00:05:36.448
nuclear power argue that it's more efficient than other energy sources since it produces huge amounts of energy. And,

47
00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:42.194
unlike coal power plants, nuclear plants don't pollute the atmosphere. Still, because of concerns about the environment and

48
00:05:42.195 --> 00:05:48.857
people's health, others protest its use. They fear that a catastrophe like Chernobyl will be repeated, and point

49
00:05:48.858 --> 00:06:01.600
out another big problem with nuclear energy: its waste remains radioactive for thousands of years, and there is no

50
00:06:01.601 --> 00:06:09.754
easy way to dispose of it. The accident at the Chernobyl plant has led to better safety practices

51
00:06:09.755 --> 00:06:14.413
at nuclear power plants, along with construction of better equipment. But debates about nuclear energy are likely to

52
00:06:14.414 --> 00:06:21.249
continue for many years. And the disaster at Chernobyl, with its millions of victims, is a chilling reminder of

53
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.574
the dangers involved. An Environmental Miracle Today Pripyat is a ghost town, empty except for abandoned buildings and

54
00:06:23.575 --> 00:06:31.728
possessions left behind by former residents. Inside homes calendar pages are still open to April 1986. Toys, clothes,

55
00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:47.059
and photographs still lay scattered across floors. It is as though the entire town has been frozen in time.

56
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:54.466
The Chernobyl plant is now encased in concrete, and the last reactor at the plant was shut down

57
00:06:54.467 --> 00:07:04.032
in 2000. The area around the plant is known as the exclusion zone, and radioactivity lingers there, in

58
00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:09.444
fields, forests, and deserted towns. Experts are unsure of how long it will take for this zone to become

59
00:07:09.445 --> 00:07:15.611
fully safe again. But the world's worst environmental disaster has also turned into an environmental miracle. After the

60
00:07:15.612 --> 00:07:25.436
towns in the exclusion zone were abandoned, grass and weeds slowly began covering buildings and streets. Trees sprang

61
00:07:25.437 --> 00:07:32.261
up everywhere. Today people regularly spot birds and other animals such as wolves, horses, and moose. To everyone's amazement

62
00:07:32.262 --> 00:07:42.756
the radioactive area around Chernobyl is now one of Europe's largest wildlife sanctuaries—a wilderness blooming with new life.

